  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 214 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nAdditional low level moisture will wrap back into the region \novernight on the back side of the low, which will reintroduce \nlow clouds and perhaps some drizzle by tomorrow morning. Dry, \nwesterly winds will gradually bring in cooler temperatures and \nscour this moisture through the day tomorrow, allowing skies to\nbecome more broken or scattered as we approach sunset, \nresulting in notably cooler temperatures tomorrow night and \nThursday morning. \n\nOn Thursday, an upper trough will traverse the middle CONUS, driving \nanother cold front through the Middle Mississippi Valley. Guidance \nappears to be in relatively good consensus on frontal timing, with \nthe front arriving in our northern CWA around midday and pushing \nthrough the CWA by late evening. Moisture return ahead of the front \nlooks fairly weak, so precipitation chances along the front are \nfairly low (< 25%). If it does rain, amounts will be light, given \nthe limited moisture. What will be more notable is that temperatures \nare expected to drop behind the front, with the current forecast \ncalling for temperatures around seasonal normals. \n\nUpper level ridging and surface high pressure will take hold on \nFriday and Saturday. This will result in gradually clearing \nskies and weakening winds. These factors, coupled with the \ncooler and drier airmass in pace behind the late-week fropa, \nwill set the stage for at least some areas within CWA to approach\nof fall below freezing, especially on Sunday morning.",
